      "content": "Those peculiar internal patterns can be quite puzzling. While I couldn't find any specific, formal classification for these types of localized, unexplained patterns, there are some related concepts and terminology that might be relevant.\n\nIn the forestry and wood science communities, these types of anomalies are sometimes referred to as \"internal defects\" or \"internal irregularities.\" They can be caused by a variety of factors, including natural defects, processing errors, or even the effects of environmental stressors.\n\nOne concept that might be relevant is the idea of \"internal checks\" or \"internal checks and scars.\" These are small, localized areas of wood that have been damaged or deformed during the tree's growth, often as a result of wind, water, or other environmental stressors. Internal checks can be caused by a variety of factors, including fungal infections, insect damage, or even the effects of wind or water damage.\n\nAnother concept that might be relevant is the idea of \"wood decay patterns.\" Wood decay can be caused by a variety of factors, including fungi, bacteria, and insects. However, some types of decay patterns can be quite unusual and may not fit neatly into traditional categories.\n\nIn terms of formal documentation, there are some industry publications and standards that discuss internal",
